                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🎯 Chapter 1 Exam Prep — OSI &amp; TCP/IP Models</h5><p><strong>Exam Weight: HIGH (15-20% of questions)</strong></p><div class="card-grid"><div class="info-card"><h5>📋 Must Memorize</h5><ul><li>7 OSI layers bottom-up: Physical→Data Link→Network→Transport→Session→Presentation→Application</li><li>PDU per layer: Bits→Frames→Packets→Segments→Data</li><li>TCP/IP 4 layers: Link→Internet→Transport→Application</li><li>Mnemonic: <strong>P</strong>lease <strong>D</strong>o <strong>N</strong>ot <strong>T</strong>hrow <strong>S</strong>ausage <strong>P</strong>izza <strong>A</strong>way</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>⚠️ Common Traps</h5><ul><li>"Ping works = network is fine" — ICMP only tests L1-L3!</li><li>Router=L3 (by IP), Switch=L2 (by MAC), Hub=L1 (repeat bits)</li><li>TLS is L6 (Presentation), NOT L7</li><li>Bottom-up debugging order is ALWAYS tested</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>🔧 Quick Commands</h5><ul><li><code>ping</code> — L3 reachability</li><li><code>traceroute</code> — Path discovery</li><li><code>ss -tlnp</code> — L4 port check</li><li><code>curl -v</code> — L7 app test</li><li><code>tcpdump -i any -nn</code> — All layers</li></ul></div></div></div>
'@
    '2' = @'
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🎯 Chapter 2 Exam Prep — Physical Layer &amp; Ethernet</h5><p><strong>Exam Weight: MEDIUM (10% of questions)</strong></p><div class="card-grid"><div class="info-card"><h5>📋 Must Memorize</h5><ul><li>Ethernet frame: Preamble(8B)+DstMAC(6B)+SrcMAC(6B)+EthType(2B)+Payload(46-1500B)+FCS(4B)</li><li>EthType values: 0x0800=IPv4, 0x0806=ARP, 0x86DD=IPv6, 0x8100=VLAN</li><li>CSMA/CD: Carrier Sense → Multiple Access → Collision Detection</li><li>Switch 3 Fs: Flood(unknown), Forward(known), Filter(same port)</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>⚠️ Common Traps</h5><ul><li>"Link UP" does NOT mean link is healthy — check error counters!</li><li>Duplex mismatch = collisions on one side, late collisions on the other</li><li>VXLAN overhead (50B) reduces effective MTU → PMTU black holes</li><li>Auto-negotiation is MANDATORY at 1 Gbps and above</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>🔧 Quick Commands</h5><ul><li><code>ethtool eth0</code> — Speed, duplex, link state</li><li><code>ip -s link show eth0</code> — Error counters</li><li><code>ip link show eth0</code> — MTU, state, MAC</li><li><code>bridge fdb show br br0</code> — MAC table</li><li><code>arping -I eth0 IP</code> — L2 reachability</li></ul></div></div></div>
'@
    '3' = @'
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🎯 Chapter 3 Exam Prep — ARP, MAC &amp; Switching</h5><p><strong>Exam Weight: MEDIUM (8-10% of questions)</strong></p><div class="card-grid"><div class="info-card"><h5>📋 Must Memorize</h5><ul><li>ARP: Broadcast request "Who has IP X?" → Unicast reply with MAC</li><li>ARP table cached (ip neigh), entries time out (default ~60s stale)</li><li>Gratuitous ARP: unsolicited ARP for duplicate IP detection &amp; MAC update</li><li>Proxy ARP: router answers ARP on behalf of remote hosts</li><li>MAC types: Unicast(bit0=0), Multicast(bit0=1), Broadcast(ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff)</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>⚠️ Common Traps</h5><ul><li>ARP spoofing/poisoning = MITM attack — use DAI or static ARP</li><li>Stale ARP entries cause "network works, then breaks" issues</li><li>ARP only works within the same L2 broadcast domain</li><li>ip neigh != arp command (ip is the modern replacement)</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>🔧 Quick Commands</h5><ul><li><code>ip neigh show</code> — View ARP table</li><li><code>arping -I eth0 192.168.1.5</code> — Test ARP</li><li><code>ip neigh add/del</code> — Static ARP</li><li><code>tcpdump -i eth0 arp</code> — Capture ARP traffic</li></ul></div></div></div>
