                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🎯 Chapter 15 Exam Prep — NAT &amp; Port Forwarding</h5><p><strong>Exam Weight: MEDIUM (8-10% of questions)</strong></p><div class="card-grid"><div class="info-card"><h5>📋 Must Memorize</h5><ul><li>SNAT/Masquerade: changes SRC IP (POSTROUTING chain)</li><li>DNAT: changes DST IP (PREROUTING chain)</li><li>PAT/NAPT: NAT + port translation (most common home/office NAT)</li><li>Full-cone, Restricted-cone, Port-restricted, Symmetric NAT types</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>⚠️ Common Traps</h5><ul><li>NAT breaks end-to-end connectivity principle of the Internet</li><li>Conntrack table overflow = new connections dropped (monitor size!)</li><li>NAT hairpinning: internal client accessing public IP of internal server</li><li>Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT): 100.64.0.0/10 — double NAT scenarios</li></ul></div><div class="info-card"><h5>🔧 Quick Commands</h5><ul><li><code>conntrack -L</code> — Connection tracking</li><li><code>iptables -t nat -L -nv</code> — NAT rules</li><li><code>cat /proc/net/nf_conntrack</code> — Conntrack table</li></ul></div></div></div>
